{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793463401,"nonce":"edwB/qaS1r8q7tprhXomrFq18Mo=","title":"Tiered: a loop's promotion request call keeps its live values on the stack","message":"With an optimizing tier above (ZYLUA_LLVM=1, ZYPY_LLVM=1), tier-0 code counts each loop header's visits and calls `__zyntax_osr_request` at the threshold, then goes on with the loop. A call that returns into the loop makes the register allocator treat every caller-saved register as clobbered across the values live through it; on aarch64 no 128-bit vector register survives a call, so lambda_call's eight vectorised accumulators were reloaded and stored on every trip (46 q-register loads and stores in main, none without the tier above). The transfer path does not have the problem, since it returns instead of continuing, as its comment says; the request added later does.\n\nMeasured on the Mac, same binary, LLVM tier on vs Cranelift alone (wall ms): lambda_call 16.8 vs 13.5 (elapsed 4.6 vs 1.4), closure_call 22.6 vs 19.1, free_function_call 30.9 vs 25.8, inlined_call 30.0 vs 25.5; branchy 101.2 vs 77.8 and collatz 244.9 vs 170.5 for another reason (LLVM's code turns their unpredictable branches into selects on a long dependency chain), records and tokenize also slower with the tier.\n\nFix: the request goes through a trampoline that saves every caller-saved register (the full vector registers on aarch64, the xmm registers on x86_64) around the C call, and the probe calls it with Cranelift's preserve_all convention, so the call clobbers nothing. Windows keeps the plain call.","files":null}]}